NAME
    tail - print the last lines of a file

SYNOPSIS
    tail [-N] [FILE]
    tail [FILE]

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the last 10 lines of FILE (default: stdin). With -N
    (e.g. -5), prints the last N lines.

    No -f (follow) flag yet — tail reads the file to EOF, finds the
    last N newlines, and exits. For log-following, you'll need to
    poll the file size in a loop or wait for the future -f flag.

    Inside a `|>` structured pipeline, tail silently consumes the
    --advjson flag.

EXAMPLES
    tail /var/log/build.log
    tail -1 /etc/passwd                 # last line
    ps | tail -3                        # last 3 processes by pid

EXIT STATUS
    0   lines were printed
    1   FILE could not be opened

SEE ALSO
    head(1), cat(1), grep(1)
